+## 1a. Psalm 141:2 as an Evening Prayer Sentence — a 1928 American selection, absent from 1662
+
+*(Added 260835-39, 2026-08-29. ⛔⛔ **EXTERNAL RESEARCH ONLY, like the rest of this document — NOT a finding about Rev. James, and no conclusion is drawn from it.** Recorded here rather than in the findings corpus for exactly that reason. Cross-referenced from `St_Francis_EMC_Distinctives.md` `LS-139`, whose `File 84` material raised the question.)*
+
+**The fact, and its dating.** The verse *"Let my prayer be set forth in thy sight as the incense; and let the lifting up of my hands be an evening sacrifice"* (Psalm 141:2) is one of the **appointed opening Sentences of Scripture at the Order for Daily Evening Prayer in the 1928 American Book of Common Prayer**, printed on **p. 21**, the office's first page, as the third Sentence in the list and carrying its own citation line *"Psalm cxli. 2."* The rubric immediately above it reads: *"The Minister shall begin the Evening Prayer by reading one or more of the following Sentences of Scripture."*
+
+**It is a Sentence, not a versicle** — a distinction the book itself draws on that same page, whose following rubric directs the Minister *"after the Sentences, [to] pass to the Versicles, O Lord open thou our lips, etc."* In the 1928 the Sentences **precede** the Versicles; they are not alternatives to them.
+
+**What 1662 has instead.** The 1662 Book of Common Prayer's Order for Evening Prayer opens with its own set of Sentences under a closely parallel rubric — *"At the beginning of Evening Prayer the Minister shall read with a loud voice one or more of these Sentences of the Scriptures that follow"* — and **Psalm 141:2 is not among them.** The 1662 set is uniformly **penitential**, and is the same set used at Morning Prayer: Ezekiel 18:27; Psalm 51:3; Psalm 51:9; Psalm 51:17; Joel 2:13; Daniel 9:9–10; Jeremiah 10:24; St Matthew 3:2; and the rest.
+
+**So the 1928 book's inclusion of Psalm 141:2 at Evening Prayer is a compilers' selection, not something inherited unchanged from 1662.** ⛔ **That is the whole of the claim made here. No inference is drawn about why it was selected, what it implies about the 1928 compilers' ceremonial intentions, or what it licenses for anyone using the 1928 book. Recording the selection and its dating is the entire purpose of this section.**
+
+**Where Psalm 141 does occur in 1662, stated precisely so the absence above is not overread.** A whole-book search of the 1662 returns **exactly one** occurrence of the phrase *"lifting up of my hands"*: in the **Psalter**, at Psalm 141, where the verse reads (orthography modernised from the long ſ) — *"Let my prayer be set forth in thy sight **as the incense**: and let the lifting up of my hands be an evening sacrifice."* The 1662 Psalter and the 1928 Sentence are therefore **verbally identical apart from one mark of punctuation** — 1662 has a **colon** at the caesura, 1928 a **semicolon**; both are the Coverdale Psalter text. ⚠️ **The point is not that 1662 lacks the words. It is that 1662 does not place them in the office.**
+
+⚠️ **One limit on the above, flagged rather than resolved.** Psalm 141 sits in the 1662 Psalter's **Day 29** group (the monthly course runs Pss 139–143 on Day 29, Pss 144–150 on Day 30). Whether the book assigns it to Morning or to Evening Prayer within that day **could not be established from this artifact** — the registered PDF's text layer carries the *"The 29. Day."* running heads but no Morning/Evening division marker at this point. ⛔ **So no claim is made here about whether a 1662 user would ever meet Psalm 141 at Evening Prayer in the monthly course; only that the 1662 does not appoint it as an Evening Prayer Sentence.**
+
+**Sources — both primary, both now in the repository.** `src/the-book-of-common-prayer-1928.pdf`, printed p. 21 (PDF p. 28); `src/the-book-of-common-prayer-1662.pdf`, Evening Prayer Sentences and Psalter Ps 141 (PDF p. 303). Both registered in `SRC_Manifest.md` §External Primary Texts at `260835-39`, unnumbered per JD's `260835-35` class-wide ruling. ⭐ **Both verified directly by this pass against the page images, and independently verified by JD against his own copies before the pass ran.**
